const DIVE_CONTRACT = (path) => `OUTPUT FILE: ${path}
Write it with the Write tool; create parent directories as needed.

Structure, in this order:
1. YAML frontmatter: title, topic, agent (your label), model: sonnet, date_researched: ${DATE}, sources_count, fixtures (the fixture directory you used), scope (2-3 lines on what is and is not covered).
2. A table of contents.
3. "## Summary" — 10-20 bullet lines, each a standalone actionable claim.
4. "## Findings" — numbered subsections. EVERY non-obvious claim carries an inline citation as a markdown link to the exact URL read, or a repo@sha12:path:line into the exemplar corpus. Correct and incorrect code side by side wherever a rule is easier shown than told. Exact builtins, options, parameter-expansion forms, shopt/setopt names, SC codes, PSScriptAnalyzer rule names, cmdlet parameters, command lines and numeric thresholds, each with the shell/tool version it applies to.
5. "## Normative guidance candidates" — numbered, crisp, checkable imperative rules distilled from the findings, each with: the rule, a one-line rationale, how a reviewer VERIFIES it (a shellcheck code with the dialect/optional check that enables it, a PSScriptAnalyzer rule with its settings, a matrix.sh run, a shfmt option, a grep with an explicit directory operand, or a named reading heuristic), and whether that verification was RUN against a planted violation (yes, with the fixture path / no, reading heuristic only). This is the section that matters most — make it dense and specific.
6. "## Verification runs" — for every runnable verification: fixture path, command, exit code on the violation, exit code on the compliant twin, the relevant output lines. A verification that did not go red is reported as such, with why.
7. "## Exemplar evidence" — which exemplars satisfy, violate or contradict each candidate, with repo@sha12:path:line (from the audits or your own re-measurement).
8. "## AI-agent angle" — what an LLM characteristically gets WRONG here (bashisms under #!/bin/sh, GNU-only flags on macOS/BSD/busybox, bash 4+ features in macOS-bound scripts, set -euo pipefail cargo cult, unquoted expansions, echo -e, which, PowerShell 7-only syntax in 5.1-bound scripts, deprecated or removed nushell commands from older training data, fish 3 vs 4 differences, zsh word-splitting assumptions; hallucinated flags and cmdlets; code that runs on the author's shell but not the target) and the smallest mechanical check that catches each mistake.
9. "## Contested / evolving" — where practice genuinely disagrees or recently changed, and which way it is trending, as of when.
10. "## Sources" — table: URL | what it is | date/era | why worth reading. Minimum 12 distinct sources, at least 6 primary (POSIX.1-2024 XCU, official shell manuals, NEWS/CHANGES/changelogs/release notes, learn.microsoft.com PowerShell docs, the tool's own repository or docs, the ShellCheck wiki).

Hard requirements:
- Call ToolSearch with query "select:WebSearch,WebFetch" FIRST to load the web tools, then research. For long reference pages and GitHub-hosted files prefer 'curl -sL <url>' through Bash so you read the whole page; a local clone of the ShellCheck wiki is at ${EX}/koalaman__shellcheck.wiki.
- Actually FETCH the primary sources; never write from search snippets.
- Reflect current practice as of ${DATE} ; flag historical-only guidance with the version it stopped applying.
- No "it depends" without saying what it depends on.
- Verification commands you propose must follow this shape (a checker enforces it later): grep/rg always with an explicit directory operand ('.' at minimum) and -r where recursive; one -e per alternative instead of a pipe character inside a pattern; quote every --include glob ('--include=*.sh' must be written --include='*.sh'); never an unquoted '**'; no '$(...)' command substitution; no angle-bracket placeholders inside a search pattern; pipe into 'xargs -r'. Write the check so its OUTPUT is the violation (empty output = pass), and say so.
- Do not modify any file other than your output file and your fixture directory.`

const DIVE_SCHEMA = {
  type: 'object',
  additionalProperties: false,
  required: ['path', 'sources_count', 'verifications_run', 'top_rules', 'surprises'],
  properties: {
    path: { type: 'string' },
    sources_count: { type: 'number' },
    verifications_run: { type: 'number', description: 'how many verifications were watched going red on a planted fixture' },
    top_rules: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' }, maxItems: 10 },
    surprises: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' }, maxItems: 6, description: 'findings the brief did not anticipate and that deserve their own round' },
  },
}

const CONSOLIDATE_SCHEMA = {
  type: 'object',
  additionalProperties: false,
  required: ['path', 'rule_ids', 'conflicts_resolved', 'needs_another_round', 'must_count'],
  properties: {
    path: { type: 'string' },
    rule_ids: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' }, description: 'ID — imperative, one per rule' },
    conflicts_resolved: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
    needs_another_round: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' }, description: 'subarea + the question, one per line' },
    must_count: { type: 'number' },
  },
}

function dive(item) {
  const path = RESEARCH + '/shell-' + item.group + '/' + item.slug + '.md'
  return agent(
    `Model rationale: sonnet — online research, source digestion and fixture runs; volume work whose cost is in pages read and commands run, not in judgment.

${CONTEXT}

YOUR SUBAREA: ${item.label} (group: ${item.group_label}; rule family: ${item.id_family}; fixture dir: ${FIX}/${item.slug}/)

RESEARCH BRIEF (from the topic map; follow it exactly):
${item.brief}

${DIVE_CONTRACT(path)}

Return ONLY the structured receipt: the output path, source count, how many verifications you watched go red, your 8 strongest normative rules as one-liners, and up to 6 surprises.`,
    { label: 'dive:' + item.group + '/' + item.slug, phase: 'Dive', model: 'sonnet', effort: 'high', schema: DIVE_SCHEMA },
  )
}

function consolidate(group, items) {
  const path = RESEARCH + '/shell-' + group + '.md'
  const dir = RESEARCH + '/shell-' + group + '/'
  const families = [...new Set(items.map(i => i.id_family))]
  const family = families.join(' + ')
  const label = items[0].group_label
  const revise = EXISTING.has(group)
  const task = revise
    ? `You are REVISING an existing consolidated artifact to fold in a follow-up round it itself commissioned.

Read ${path} IN FULL, then every file in ${dir} in full — including the new artifact(s) this round produced: ${items.map(i => i.slug + '.md').join(', ')}.

Rewrite ${path} in place, preserving its structure:
- ID STABILITY IS A HARD CONTRACT. Every existing rule ID keeps its number and its meaning. Do not renumber, do not reorder into different IDs, do not reuse a retired number.
- New rules get NEW IDs continuing the sequence.
- If the follow-up CONTRADICTS an existing rule, change that rule's text in place and record the change — never leave both standing. A rule that overclaims a guarantee the new research shows does not exist is the most dangerous kind; fix it explicitly.
- A verification the new round watched FAIL to go red must be replaced or the rule demoted to a reading heuristic; record which.
- Update the Verdict for whatever the follow-up settled, and REMOVE from Open questions whatever it answered. Where the research established a GAP rather than an answer, move it into the Verdict as a documented gap.
- Add or extend a "## Revision log": one line per change — what, which IDs, why.
- Update the frontmatter's consolidates list and add a revised: ${DATE} key.`
    : `You are consolidating a topic into the single authoritative artifact a later author works from.

Read EVERY file in ${dir} (list it first; read each in full), plus the seven audits under ${RESEARCH}/shell-audit/ (at least the sections relevant to this group) and the topic map's rows for this group.

Write ${path}:
1. YAML frontmatter: title, topic, model: sonnet, id_family: ${families.join(', ')}, consolidates (the file list), date: ${DATE}.
2. "## Verdict" — 5-15 lines: the position this program takes, stated as DECISIONS, not as a survey. Where sub-researchers disagreed, decide and say why. Name the script kind each decision binds (installer/bootstrap, CI step, dev tooling script, library sourced by others, interactive rc/integration code, emitted code) when it differs.
3. "## The ruleset" — merged, de-duplicated, numbered rules, one subsection per ID family this group owns (${families.join(', ')}). Each entry: ID (<FAMILY>-nn, e.g. SH-ERR-01), the rule as an imperative sentence, rationale (one line — the failure it prevents), verification (exact shellcheck code with dialect/optional check, PSScriptAnalyzer rule with settings, matrix.sh run with the family, shfmt option, or grep with a directory operand; or a named reading heuristic), whether the verification was watched going red on a planted fixture (cite the dive's Verification runs), severity (MUST / SHOULD / CONSIDER), and the shell/version/OS scope it binds (e.g. 'all POSIX sh', 'bash scripts that may run on macOS', 'PowerShell scripts that must run on 5.1'). A MUST needs either a normative or measured source AND a verification that was run, or an explicit statement why it can only be a reading heuristic. Drop rules that are generic model common knowledge and would not change behaviour; keep the ones an agent gets wrong without being told. Smallest set with the highest yield. Group rows by the check that catches them.
4. "## Applied to the fleet and exemplars" — which rules the fleet and strict exemplars already satisfy, which they violate (file:line or repo@sha12:path:line from the audits or sub-artifacts), and which are new commitments for the fleet (ocx emitter, installers, scripts, CI).
5. "## AI-agent failure modes" — merged, ranked by how often it bites, each with the mechanical check.
6. "## Open questions" — what needs an owner decision (with the default the program will apply), and which subarea deserves ANOTHER research round (name the subarea and the question).
7. "## Sub-artifacts" — relative markdown links to each shell-${group}/<file>.md with a one-line description.
8. "## Key sources" — the 10-15 best URLs across all sub-artifacts.

Merge rules: no claim without a citation that traces to a sub-artifact or a URL; no duplicated rules; prefer the more specific formulation; when two sub-artifacts conflict, state the conflict and resolve it with a reason. A consolidation with no resolved conflict and no "violated" row is a survey wearing a verdict's clothes — do not produce one.`

  return agent(
    `Model rationale: sonnet at xhigh effort — synthesis across conflicting sources plus normative decisions that later become enforced rules; the user's routing policy raises effort on sonnet before escalating to opus.

${CONTEXT}

TOPIC: ${label} (family ${family})

${task}

You may re-run a sub-artifact's recorded verification with ${RUN} or ${MATRIX} to settle a conflict. Do not modify any file other than ${path}.

Return ONLY the structured receipt: path, rule IDs with one-line imperatives, conflicts resolved, subareas needing another round, and the MUST count.`,
    { label: (revise ? 'revise:' : 'consolidate:') + group, phase: 'Consolidate', model: 'sonnet', effort: 'xhigh', schema: CONSOLIDATE_SCHEMA },
  )
}

